Name: show in finder

Synonyms: show in explorer

Type: command

Syntax: show <pathname> in {finder | explorer}

Summary:
Opens the folder that holds a file or folder in the desktop's file
manager, with the file or folder selected.

Introduced: 9.7.1

OS: mac, windows, linux

Platforms: desktop

Security: disk, process

Example:
show "/Users/me/Documents/report.pdf" in finder

Example:
show tExportedFile in explorer
if the result is not empty then
   answer "Could not show the file:" && the result
end if

Parameters:
pathname:
The name and location of a file or folder. If you specify a name but
not a location, the engine looks for it in the <defaultFolder>.

The result:
Empty when the file manager was asked to show it. If there is no file
or folder at <pathname>, the result is "file not found". If no file
manager could be started (on a phone or a server, or on a Linux system
with none), the result is "can't show that file".

Description:
Use the <show in finder> <command> to point the user at a file your
stack wrote, or one they asked about, in the file manager they know.

"finder" and "explorer" are the same command on every system: the
Finder opens on macOS, File Explorer on Windows, and on Linux the
desktop's own file manager (asked over D-Bus, as other applications
ask it, or started with the file selected), or failing that whatever
opens the folder that holds the file.

It starts another program, so it needs both the disk and the processes
that the <secureMode> takes away.

The path is never an object's reference: "show button 1 in finder"
shows the button and stops, as it did before this command came. A
variable that holds an object's name or long id is read as a path like
any other, so "show tObject in finder" gives "file not found", where
earlier versions showed the object and then stopped with an error.

>*Note:* "finder" and "explorer" are no words of the language. A
> variable, control or custom property with either name means what it
> always did, and <show> reads them this way only after "in" at the end
> of the command. This command is new in OXT-Beyond 0.2.8-dp.3, from
> HyperXTalk, where Emily-Elizabeth Howard wrote it. In earlier versions
> "show <pathname> in finder" stopped the script with an error.

References: show (command), move file to trash (command),
launch document (command), defaultFolder (property),
secureMode (property), command (glossary)

Tags: file system
